WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.094
A repair platform contains ticketing, technician
scheduling and customer notifications in one

00:00:06.094 --> 00:00:07.601
deployable application.

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:13.171
Running four copies behind a load balancer creates
four replicas of that application.

00:00:13.171 --> 00:00:15.924
It does not create four business services.

00:00:15.924 --> 00:00:22.411
Each replica can still execute all three
responsibilities, and a release typically deploys
the same

00:00:22.411 --> 00:00:23.722
application package.

00:00:23.722 --> 00:00:30.059
A database and cache outside that package do not, on
their own, establish independent application

00:00:30.059 --> 00:00:30.778
boundaries.

00:00:30.778 --> 00:00:37.115
This arrangement can be useful for a small team:
developers run one application locally, follow a

00:00:37.115 --> 00:00:42.669
request through one process and use a local database
transaction for related changes.

00:00:42.669 --> 00:00:49.072
Trouble may appear when unrelated modules force
coordinated releases or compete for very different

00:00:49.072 --> 00:00:49.725
resources.

00:00:49.725 --> 00:00:54.869
First ask whether clearer modules inside the
application would address the problem.

00:00:54.869 --> 00:01:00.880
A network boundary adds deployment and failure
concerns, so justify it using an observed need for

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.961
independent change or operation rather than the
visual appeal of additional boxes.

00:01:05.961 --> 00:01:11.945
Suppose technician scheduling becomes a separate
service because its optimization workload and

00:01:11.945 --> 00:01:14.874
release cadence differ from ticket management.

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:21.049
The boundary now has a contract: ticket management
requests a slot, scheduling accepts or rejects

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:24.041
it, and both agree on identifiers and outcomes.

00:01:24.041 --> 00:01:30.407
This can allow independent implementation, but
independence is weakened if every schema change
still

00:01:30.407 --> 00:01:33.017
forces both services to release together.

00:01:33.061 --> 00:01:36.501
A meaningful boundary contains change as well as
code.

00:01:36.501 --> 00:01:39.814
Now scheduling accepts a slot but the reply is lost.

00:01:39.814 --> 00:01:44.656
Ticket management sees a timeout while scheduling
has already changed state.

00:01:44.656 --> 00:01:50.007
A local rollback in ticket management cannot undo a
remote commitment automatically.

00:01:50.007 --> 00:01:55.932
The design needs a way to discover the outcome,
avoid duplicate reservations and recover from

00:01:55.932 --> 00:01:56.888
abandoned work.

00:01:56.888 --> 00:02:01.448
Trace this failure before celebrating a successful
normal-path demo.

00:02:01.448 --> 00:02:07.753
Smaller services can make focused tests easier,
while cross-service behavior requires contract

00:02:07.753 --> 00:02:11.240
tests, observability and explicit recovery policies.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:15.734
Compare these costs with the independence that
motivated the split.
